<title>BELGIUM: EU shows outstanding trade growth with 'three Chinas'.</title>
<headline>EU shows outstanding trade growth with 'three Chinas'.</headline>
<byline>David Fox</byline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-06-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>European Union trade with the &quot;three Chinas&quot; -- mainland China, Hong Kong and Taiwan -- has soared this decade, showing growth of over 16 percent a year since 1990.</p>
<p>Eurostat, the EU's statistical office, said on Tuesday that exports from the 15-nation bloc to China, Taiwan and Hong Kong rose from 18.2 billion European currency units (ecus) in 1990 to 41.9 billion last year.</p>
<p>Imports rose from 27.9 billion ecus to 50.1 billion, leaving the EU some 8.14 billion in the red. The trade deficit in 1990 was 9.8 billion ecus.</p>
<p>Year-on-year, EU imports from Hong Kong, China and Taiwan last year rose 0.3 percent, 13.8 percent and 9.7 percent respectively.</p>
<p>Exports to Hong Kong and China rose 9.8 and 0.2 percent, but fell 1.7 percent to Taiwan.</p>
<p>Manufactured goods -- chiefly machinery and transport equipment -- accounted for nearly 90 percent of EU exports to Hongkong and also for over 31 percent of imports from the British colony.</p>
<p>Electrical goods and automatic data-processing equipment were the biggest EU imports, accounting for nearly two-thirds of the total. Clothing and accessories accounted for nearly 35 percent.</p>
